\begin{proof}
Consider the singularity of $f$ at infinity, equivalently the singularity at $0$ of $g(z)=f(1/z)$. If this singularity were essential, then by the Great Picard theorem, in every punctured neighborhood of $0$, $g$ would take every complex value, with at most one exception, infinitely often. Hence $f$ would take some value infinitely often, contradicting injectivity. Thus the singularity at infinity is removable or a pole.

If it is removable, then $f$ is bounded outside a sufficiently large disk and, being continuous on the remaining compact disk, is bounded on all of $\mathbb C$. Liouville's theorem would imply that $f$ is constant, contradicting injectivity. Therefore infinity is a pole, so $f$ is a polynomial.

Write $f(z)=a_nz^n+\cdots+a_0$ with $a_n\ne0$. If $n\ge2$, then for any $w$ that is not a critical value, the polynomial $f(z)-w$ has $n$ distinct roots by the fundamental theorem of algebra, contradicting injectivity. Hence $n=1$, and therefore $f(z)=az+b$ with $a\ne0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $g(\zeta)=f(1/\zeta)$ on $\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$. Suppose that $g$ has an essential singularity at $0$. By the Casorati--Weierstrass theorem, $g$ has dense image in every punctured neighborhood of $0$. Choose $z_0\ne0$. Since $f$ is nonconstant and holomorphic, the open mapping theorem gives a neighborhood $U$ of $z_0$ such that $f(U)$ is open. Density of $g$ implies that, for a value $w\in f(U)$, there are points $\zeta$ arbitrarily close to $0$ with $g(\zeta)=w$. These yield points $1/\zeta$ outside $U$ with the same image as a point of $U$, contradicting injectivity. Thus $g$ is not essential at $0$.

If the singularity is removable, then $f$ is bounded near infinity. Since $f$ is bounded on every closed disk, it is bounded on $\mathbb C$, so Liouville's theorem makes it constant, impossible for an injective function. Consequently $g$ has a pole at $0$, and $f$ is a polynomial.

If $f$ has degree $n\ge2$, then $f'$ is nonconstant and has a zero $c$ by the fundamental theorem of algebra. Choose a small disk centered at $c$ containing no other zero of $f'$. By the local behavior of a holomorphic map at a critical point, $f$ is not one-to-one on that disk, contradicting injectivity. Hence $\deg f=1$, so $f(z)=az+b$ with $a\ne0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
We prove first that $f$ is a polynomial. If infinity were an essential singularity of $f$, then $g(\zeta)=f(1/\zeta)$ would have an essential singularity at $0$. By the Great Picard theorem, some complex value would be assumed by $g$ infinitely many times in every punctured neighborhood of $0$. Equivalently, $f$ would assume that value at infinitely many distinct points of $\mathbb C$, contradicting injectivity.

Thus infinity is removable or a pole. In the removable case, $f$ is bounded near infinity; together with boundedness on a sufficiently large closed disk, this makes $f$ bounded on $\mathbb C$. Liouville's theorem then says that $f$ is constant, again a contradiction. Hence infinity is a pole, and therefore $f$ is a polynomial.

Let $n=\deg f$. If $n\ge2$, choose $w$ so that $f(z)-w$ has no repeated root; such a $w$ exists because the repeated-root values are among the finitely many values $f(c)$ with $f'(c)=0$. The fundamental theorem of algebra then gives $n\ge2$ distinct solutions of $f(z)=w$, contradicting injectivity. Therefore $n=1$, and $f(z)=az+b$ for some $a,b\in\mathbb C$, with $a\ne0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Regard $f$ as a holomorphic function at infinity via $g(\zeta)=f(1/\zeta)$. The singularity of $g$ at $0$ cannot be essential: if it were, the Great Picard theorem would imply that some value is taken infinitely often near $0$, so $f$ would take that value at infinitely many distinct points, contrary to injectivity.

It also cannot be removable. Indeed, removability would make $f$ bounded outside a disk. Since $f$ is continuous, it is bounded on the disk as well, and hence bounded on all of $\mathbb C$. Liouville's theorem would then make $f$ constant, contradicting injectivity. Therefore $g$ has a pole at $0$, which means that $f$ is a polynomial.

Let $n=\deg f$. If $n\ge2$, then $f'$ has a zero, and the finite set of critical values $f(\{z:f'(z)=0\})$ cannot equal all of $\mathbb C$. Choose $w$ outside this set. Every root of $f(z)-w$ is then simple. By the fundamental theorem of algebra, $f(z)-w$ has exactly $n$ roots counted with multiplicity, hence at least two distinct roots. This contradicts injectivity. Thus $n=1$.

Consequently $f(z)=az+b$ with $a,b\in\mathbb C$ and $a\ne0$.
\end{proof}
